# ROW 4
d.R('- The dwelling chain runs on to 3:16',"- 2:9 and 3:16: see **v1.2** below.")
d.R('*Confidence:* moderate–high on wording; moderate on function.',"""**v1.2** `[S: audit 5]`:
- **1:19.** Ps 67:17 LXX is the only verse in Swete where εὐδοκέω ("be pleased") and κατοικέω ("dwell") have God as subject together (reproduced in this pass). *Moderate–high*, as an echo of Zion as God's chosen dwelling.
- **1:19 and 2:9** are one formula: ἐν αὐτῷ … πᾶν τὸ πλήρωμα … κατοικ- ("in him … all the fullness … dwell"). *High* as repetition.
- **The temple overtone is *moderate*.** κατοικέω has God as subject in about 16 of its 569 Swete verses, and κατοικητήριον ("dwelling-place") is God's in 11 of 19, most of them heaven (1 Kgs 8; 2 Chr 6).
- **3:16 is indwelling, not temple (*low* as temple).** ἐνοικέω ("dwell in") never has God as subject in Swete (33 verses), and 3:16 has no temple vocabulary. At 2 Cor 6:16 the verb is Paul's own choice (Lev 26:11–12 has ἐμπεριπατήσω, "walk among"; Ezek 37:27 has κατασκήνωσις, "dwelling"), which supports indwelling more than temple. The nearest parallel to 3:16 is Eph 3:17, "that Christ may dwell (κατοικῆσαι) in your hearts". This corrects the inference made in Logos round 5 §14.5.
- **History.** Pao at 1:19: "Christ fulfills the role of the temple", with Ps 68 as a transfer of God's presence from Sinai to Zion, "now from Zion to Christ himself". He does not join 3:16 to 1:19, and Beale's *Temple* is silent on 3:16 `[S: Logos 5]`.

*Confidence:* moderate–high at 1:19; moderate on the temple overtone. A sermon may use the temple theme at 1:19 and 2:9, framed as "the nearest wording within a wider tradition" `[S: audit 1]`, and should not extend it to 3:16.""")
# ROW 13
d.B('**14. Isaiah 43:25',"""**v1.2 — the formula, and the direction.** In Deut 9:26–29 and 32:9 Israel *is* God's portion; at Col 1:12 believers *receive* one. So the nearer wording is the receiving formula μερὶς καὶ κλῆρος ("portion and lot": Deut 10:9; 12:12; 18:1), and **Acts 8:21 and Col 1:12 are the only NT verses with both words** (SBLGNT, reproduced) `[S: audit 5]`. Queued as C74. History: the holy ones as believers, Wright, Pao, Dunn and Osborne; open to angels, Bird; Wis 5:5, Dunn and Witherington; Deut 32:9, Pao, Osborne and Brown; Ps 15:5 LXX, none `[S: Logos 5]`.

*Confidence, unchanged:* moderate (idiom).
""")
# ROW 15
d.I("Acts' \"the word of God grew\" (Acts 6:7; 12:24; 19:20) is a rival `[S: audit 4]`.","Acts' \"the word of God grew\" (Acts 6:7; 12:24; 19:20) is a rival `[S: audit 4]`.\n\n**v1.2.** This is sower tradition, not a Lukan contact: καρποφορέω and ὑπομονή stand in different Colossian verses (1:6, 10; 1:11), and \"bear fruit … hear … word\" is in all three Synoptics `[S: audit 5]`. Holders: the sower, Dunn (Mark 4:8) and Pao (Luke 8:15, with the Acts rivals); Genesis, Wright, Pao, Bird and Osborne `[S: Logos 5]`. Four holders do not lift Genesis from *uncertain*, which rests on the wording.")
# ROW 16
d.R('**16. The exodus redemption',"**16. The exodus redemption, anchored in Exod 6:6–8 → 1:12–14 \"who rescued us from the domain of darkness and transferred us … in whom we have redemption\".** *Row added by audit 4; restated by audit 5.* The vocabulary of 1:12–14 — rescue from a hostile authority, transfer, redemption, an allotted lot — is exodus-shaped. Each of these words occurs once in the letter (1:1–14 dig `[S: dig]`).")
d.R('*Rarity and rival.* ῥύομαι ("rescue") with λυτρόω',"""*Rarity and rival (v1.2)* `[S: audit 5]`. **ῥύομαι ("rescue") is not an exodus marker in itself:** of its 184 Swete verses, 9.2 % stand in an exodus context, against a baseline of 9.7 %. λυτρόω ("redeem") runs at about twice the baseline (19.8 %). Rescue with redeem within twelve verses gives 18 Old Testament clusters, and only 5 are clearly exodus-related. **Adding the allotted lot narrows it to two:**
- **Exod 6:6–8**, the one passage with the whole movement in order: ῥύσομαι ὑμᾶς ἐκ τῆς δουλίας ("I will rescue you from the slavery"), λυτρώσομαι ("I will redeem"), then the land given ἐν κλήρῳ ("as a lot"). Exod 6:6 is named at 1:12–14 by Pao and Osborne `[S: Logos 5]`;
- **Esther's Addition C** (Swete 4:17, bracketed sub-verses [8]–[10]), which reuses Deut 9 in prayer. It has rescue, authority, the portion "redeemed out of Egypt" and the lot, and no darkness or light. It is the densest Greek Old Testament match, *low–moderate* as prayer idiom (queued, C76).

Supporting:""")
d.R('- **Deut 9:26–29** joins',"- **Deut 9:26–29** joins μερίς (\"portion\"), κλῆρος (\"lot\") and λυτρόω within four verses, of the people brought out of Egypt: *low–moderate*, a prayer cluster and not a source. The direction is reversed (row 13);")
d.R('- **Exod 14:30** has',"- **Exod 14:30** contributes only the aorist ἐρρύσατο (\"rescued\").")
d.R('- **Ps 106 LXX:2–20** has',"\n**Ps 106 LXX leaves the row** (*low*). It has no Egypt, Pharaoh or sea, and its ῥύομαι takes \"distresses\" and \"corruptions\", not darkness. With Isa 42:7, 16, an equal partner named by Pao, it belongs to a \"redeemed from darkness\" idiom whose phrases go to Luke rather than Colossians: Luke 1:79 takes up the words of Ps 106:10, with the participle moved. Luke 1:68–79 is *moderate* as shared idiom with Col 1:13–14 (Presenting Situation) `[S: audit 5]`.")
d.R('In the NT, Luke 1:68–79 is, with Col 1:13–14',"**The light is missing from every text above.** The exodus tradition that supplies it is the darkness plague: Exod 10:23, with Wis 18:1–9 (\"for your holy ones there was very great light\"; the word is ὁσίοις, not Colossians' ἁγίων). Audit 4 rated Wisdom 17–18 *low* as a field, on the scan; audit 5 re-proposes the plague on its wording, as C75, *low–moderate* and unaudited. **The scan is nearly blind to this whole passage** (*What the scan can and cannot show*), so every rating here rests on direct searches.")
d.R('*Confidence:* moderate (pattern); low–moderate (Exod 6 alone).',"*Confidence:* moderate, as a pattern anchored in Exod 6:6–8 (raised from low–moderate as a single text, because the triad with κλῆρος is concentrated there) `[S: audit 5]`.")
