d.R('| 31 | 1:12–14 ↔ 2:13 |','| 31 | 1:12–14 ↔ 2:13 | the "you" → "us" shift at the two *indicative* statements of forgiveness, with scribal levelling at both. Apart from "God our Father" (1:2), every other first-person plural in the letter is apostolic, so the inclusive "we" stands only at 1:13–14 and 2:13–14. Not at 3:13, where the comparison needs "you". The move is Paul\'s habit (1 Thess 1:9–10; Gal 1:3–4; Eph 2:1–5). The NASB95 levels it at 1:12; the KJV at both | Repeats | high (pronouns, apparatus); moderate–high (inclusive "we" only here); low–moderate (as a design peculiar to Colossians) `[S: audit 5]` | 1:1–14; 2:6–15 |')
d.R('| 59 | 3:8–9 → 3:16 → 4:3 → 4:6 |','| 59 | 3:8–9 → 3:16–17 → 4:3–4 → 4:6 | a speech thread carried by a *cluster* of speech words: βλασφημία ("slander"), αἰσχρολογία ("obscene talk") and ψεύδομαι ("lie") → the word of Christ → "a door for the word" → "your word". λόγος ("word") is absent from 3:8–9 and changes sense along the thread; ἐν χάριτι (3:16; 4:6) is its most specific link; δεῖ ("must", by form) only at 4:4, 6. 3:16–4:6 is denser in λόγος than about 96 % of 24-verse Pauline stretches, though the letter as a whole runs at twice the Pauline rate. Eph 4:29–6:20 has the same sequence | Develops | high (counts); moderate (as design; Paul\'s paraenetic pattern) `[S: audit 5]` | 3:5–11; 3:12–17; 4:2–6 |')
d.R('| 62 | 1:5–6 → 3:16 → 4:6 |','| 62 | 1:5–6 → 3:16 → 4:6 | λόγος with χάρις ("the word" and "grace" heard → sung → spoken). *v1.2:* if 3:16 is "with thankfulness" (a slight lean) and 4:6 is "gracious", this is a play on χάρις, not one sense carried through `[I]` | Frames | moderate | 3:12–17; 4:2–6 |')
